{"name":"Frosted Glass","hex":"#eaf0f0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frosted","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered in frost; frosty.","Translucent, appearing to be covered in frost."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of milkshake made with ice cream."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frosted"},{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frosted glass","blocks":[{"text":"Frosted glass is produced by the sandblasting or acid etching of clear sheet glass. This creates a pitted surface on one side of the glass pane and has the effect of rendering the glass translucent by scattering the light which passes through, thus blurring images while still transmitting light. It has 10–20% opacity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/-104wiki.jpg/330px--104wiki.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frosted_glass","wikidata":"Q2878056"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrass